<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Love song · 1850 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="tr">Kâtibim</bdi> / <bdi lang="tr-Latn">Üsküdar'a Gider İken</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Turkish folk song from Istanbul whose tune is sung, with other words, from the Balkans to Bangladesh.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Turks of Istanbul; versions across the Balkans, the Arab world and South Asia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Istanbul, Turkey</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>not known; a Romanian version was published by Anton Pann in the 1850s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional, words anonymous)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katibim">Kâtibim</a>&quot;, &quot;my clerk&quot;, also called &quot;Üsküdar'a Gider İken&quot;, &quot;while going to Üsküdar&quot;, is a Turkish folk song in the form of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> türkü. It tells of someone's clerk (kâtip) on the way to Üsküdar, on the Asian shore of Istanbul. The same melody is sung, usually with entirely different words, in many countries around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and beyond, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and many places claim the tune as their own; a documentary film, Whose is this song?, and an international youth project, Everybody's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, recorded many of these versions.</p><p>The versions show how far the tune travelled across the former Ottoman lands. Anton Pann collected and published a Romanian one in the 1850s. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> it is a love song and also a hymn of resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> Mountains; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> it is a sevdalinka and a qasida; Albania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> (a recording of 1910), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> have their own, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> knows it under at least eight names, among them &quot;Little trehantiri&quot; and &quot;From a foreign place&quot;. In the Arab world the same melody carries the folk song &quot;Ya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> Iskandaria&quot; (&quot;Oh girls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>&quot;), recorded by Mohammed El-Bakkar around 1957, the Aleppine &quot;Ghazali Ghazali&quot; and a devotional qasida for Muhammad; Gloria Levy sang a multilingual Sephardic version, &quot;Fel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shara">Shara</a>&quot;. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazi-nazrul-islam">Kazi Nazrul Islam</a>, who is thought to have learned the melody while fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, set it in the 1930s to an Islamic song about Muhammad and to songs of youth and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rain.</p><p>The tune reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in the 1920s: the klezmer clarinettist Naftule Brandwein, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> since 1909, recorded it without words as &quot;Der Terk in America&quot; in 1924. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the composer Saygun ended his choral work Bir Tutam Kekik, Op. 22 (1943), with variations on it as an &quot;old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> folk song&quot;. Safiye Ayla's recording of 1949 became the model for many later ones; in 1953 Eartha Kitt and Eydie Gormé recorded it in the United States as &quot;Uska Dara – A Turkish Tale&quot;, with an English adaptation by Stella Lee. Zeki Müren's recording was also popular, and he acted in the 1968 film &quot;Kâtip (Üsküdar'a Giderken)&quot;, directed by Ülkü Erakalın, in which it plays an important part.</p><p>The song is still arranged and mixed with others: in a Turkish-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> version for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s Coke Studio (2013), in Pink Martini's Get Happy (2013), in Fazil Say's violin concerto &quot;1001 Nights in the Harem&quot;, and in 2026 with Nazrul's &quot;Room Jhoom&quot; for Coke Studio Bangla. Its tune has been compared to Boney M.'s &quot;Rasputin&quot; (1978), which the band denied, although its singer Liz Mitchell later said they had bought an old Turkish folk song from a Tunisian.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A folk song about someone's clerk (kâtip) on the road to Üsküdar. The many other-language versions set new words to the same tune: love songs, a song of resistance, devotional poems for the Prophet, a song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rain.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Turkish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Turkish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Üsküdar’a gider iken aldı da bir yağmur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As [we] were going to Üsküdar, a rain came down on us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtibimin setresi uzun eteği çamur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my clerk's frock coat, its long skirt muddy.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtip uykudan uyanmış gözleri mahmur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The clerk has woken from sleep, his eyes heavy with sleep.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtip benim ben kâtibin el ne karışır,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The clerk is mine, I am the clerk's, what business is it of strangers?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtibime kolalı da gömlek ne güzel yaraşır.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How well a starched shirt suits my clerk!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Üsküdar’a gider iken bir mendil buldum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As I was going to Üsküdar, I found a handkerchief,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Mendilimin içine de lokum doldurdum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and into my handkerchief I filled Turkish delight.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtibimi arar iken yanımda buldum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As I was looking for my clerk, I found him at my side.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtip benim ben kâtibin el ne karışır,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The clerk is mine, I am the clerk's, what business is it of strangers?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kâtibime kolalı da gömlek ne güzel yaraşır.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How well a starched shirt suits my clerk!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous Turkish folk song. Text from <a href="https://tr.wikisource.org/wiki/%C3%9Csk%C3%BCdar%E2%80%99a_Gider_%C4%B0ken" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'gider iken aldı da bir yağmur' gives no subject for 'going'; 'we' is supplied from the sense (it can equally be 'I'); 'aldı' is literally 'took', i.e. the rain caught them. The particle 'da', put in to fill the tune (1.1, 2.2, 3.2), is not translated.</li><li>line 1.2: 'setre' is the long frock coat of Ottoman officials after the reforms of Mahmud II; 'uzun eteği çamur' is literally 'its long skirt mud'.</li><li>line 1.3: 'mahmur' is the languid, heavy look of eyes just woken (or drowsy, or after drink); 'heavy with sleep' is one reading.</li><li>Kâtip: a clerk or secretary (scribe). The singer's sex is not stated in the text; 'his' for the clerk follows the Turkish context (an Ottoman clerk), as Turkish has no gendered pronoun.</li><li>line 2.1: 'el' is 'other people, strangers'; 'el ne karışır' is 'why should others meddle'.</li><li>line 3.2: 'lokum' is Turkish delight.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="958Y11aZwPU" data-title="Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken): Adnan Menderes University Turkish classical music choir"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Adnan Menderes University Turkish classical music choir</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Akustik Sahne. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=958Y11aZwPU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">De ai ști, suflețelul meu (Anton Pann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1850s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Romanian version, collected and published</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Der Terk in America (Naftule Brandwein)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1924</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental recording of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bir Tutam Kekik, Op. 22 (Saygun)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">choral variations on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Uska Dara – A Turkish Tale (Eartha Kitt; Eydie Gormé)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1953</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording with an English adaptation by Stella Lee</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tribhuboner Priyo Muhammad; Shukno Patar Nupur Paye; Rumjhum Jhum Jhum (Kazi Nazrul Islam)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Bengali songs to the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Banat Iskandaria (Mohammed El-Bakkar)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Arabic folk song to the same melody, recorded</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kâtip (Üsküdar'a Giderken), film (Ülkü Erakalın)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">film built around Zeki Müren's recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Souvenir d'Ismaïlia, Op. 100 (Camille Saint-Saëns)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody quoted in the second section</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sacred Shabbat (Loreena McKennitt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ishq Kinara – Üsküdar'a Gider Iken (Coke Studio Pakistan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Turkish-Urdu mash-up</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1001 Nights in the Harem, violin concerto, third movement (Fazil Say)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune heavily quoted</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Room Jhoom mash-up (Coke Studio Bangla)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2026</span><span class="sg-chain-h">mash-up with Nazrul's song</span></li></ul></section>
